1
00:00:27,006 --> 00:00:28,480
It carries no weapons,

2
00:00:28,481 --> 00:00:32,300
yet it's the most powerful aircraft in the world.

3
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,480
Its tail number is more prestigious than any street address

4
00:00:37,481 --> 00:00:44,280
and no executive office can match its luxury appointments.

5
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,160
For more than 50 years,

6
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:52,220
American presidents have made their grand entrance

7
00:00:52,221 --> 00:00:53,960
aboard their personal planes.

8
00:00:53,961 --> 00:00:59,640
it's been the backdrop for some of history's most dramatic moments

9
00:00:59,641 --> 00:01:06,100
what began as a propeller driven army transport

10
00:01:06,101 --> 00:01:10,100
has changed with every advance in aviation technology

11
00:01:10,101 --> 00:01:15,140
and has evolved into america's turbocharged chariot of the gods

12
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,040
it's way beyond first class

13
00:01:23,041 --> 00:01:25,500
it's Air Force One.

14
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:36,820
When people come to Washington, D.C.,

15
00:01:36,821 --> 00:01:39,000
they go to see the White House

16
00:01:39,001 --> 00:01:40,720
because it's a symbol of the presidency.

17
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:43,680
And it's a symbol of his authority.

18
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,600
I think the airplane's the same thing.

19
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:46,900
When it shows up,

20
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:48,420
he knows

21
00:01:48,421 --> 00:01:51,560
that people know it's the President of the United States.

22
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:58,960
I've always felt the two most obvious symbols of American power were the Oval Office and Air Force One.

23
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:02,440
And Air Force One is the scepter you can take with you.

24
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,040
In some ways, it's the symbol of American royalty, but it's always the symbol of American power.

25
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:12,400
There's nothing comparable to Air Force One.

26
00:02:12,401 --> 00:02:18,380
You have the capability of acting in any emergency, under any conditions,

27
00:02:18,381 --> 00:02:26,400
because the technical equipment is there just like it is in the White House itself.

28
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:31,820
Air Force One represents the United States of America.

29
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:38,080
You see that big silver airplane with a red, white, and blue flag on it, taxi up.

30
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:40,720
I mean, people really get kind of emotional about it.

31
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:44,180
It's exhilarating. It truly is.

32
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:50,000
It happened even to me when I was at the White House, being on the ground waiting for the president to come back from someplace.

33
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:51,860
There's a thrill to it.

34
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,880
It's a genuine, old-fashioned, not-to-be-ashamed thrill.

35
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:10,440
For the first 117 years of the presidency,

36
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:14,280
The chief executive was, for the most part, a homebody.

37
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:22,440
Because traveling was difficult and time-consuming, he rarely left the capital city.

38
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:27,440
With very few exceptions, the president stayed close to the seat of power.

39
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:33,860
The first president to leave the United States while still in office was Teddy Roosevelt.

40
00:03:33,861 --> 00:03:44,240
In 1906, the 26th president sailed to Central America to inspect work on the Panama Canal.

41
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:52,540
The progressive Roosevelt made presidential history again just 19 months out of office.

42
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:59,200
On October 11, 1910, at an air show in Kinloch, Missouri,

43
00:03:59,201 --> 00:04:04,120
The former president joined a stunt pilot named Arch Hoxie

44
00:04:04,121 --> 00:04:07,880
For a quick spin in his right Type B biplane

45
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:12,980
Their flight didn't make the record books

46
00:04:12,981 --> 00:04:16,860
They sputtered around the fairgrounds for only a few minutes

47
00:04:16,861 --> 00:04:20,540
But Teddy Roosevelt chalked up another presidential first

48
00:04:20,541 --> 00:04:27,160
33 years later

49
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:32,200
Franklin Roosevelt became the first president to fly while still in office.

50
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,480
At the time, the world was at war with Nazi Germany.

51
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:47,100
FDR needed to join other Allied leaders at a secret war planning conference in January 1943

52
00:04:47,101 --> 00:04:50,600
in the West African city of Casablanca.

53
00:04:50,601 --> 00:04:57,240
with Nazi U-boats prowling the Atlantic

54
00:04:57,241 --> 00:05:02,100
the Secret Service felt it would be safer for the President to travel by air

55
00:05:02,101 --> 00:05:05,100
but instead of flying a military plane

56
00:05:05,101 --> 00:05:10,480
they chartered a Boeing 314 flying boat and her civilian crew

57
00:05:10,481 --> 00:05:13,160
from Pan American World Airways

58
00:05:13,161 --> 00:05:18,240
at that time our military pilots didn't have that much

59
00:05:18,241 --> 00:05:24,400
flying experience as far as total flying hours are concerned, yet some of the senior airline

60
00:05:24,401 --> 00:05:29,940
pilots had thousands of hours and great experience in hauling passengers.

61
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:40,560
On January 11th, 1943, the president boarded the Pan Am flying boat and took off for Casablanca.

62
00:05:40,561 --> 00:05:47,440
After two overnight refueling stops

63
00:05:47,441 --> 00:05:51,100
the seaplane landed off the coast of North Africa

64
00:05:51,101 --> 00:05:58,760
Roosevelt transferred to a waiting Army C-54 transport plane

65
00:05:58,761 --> 00:06:00,840
with TWA pilots at the controls

66
00:06:00,841 --> 00:06:03,580
and completed his trip to Casablanca

67
00:06:03,581 --> 00:06:08,560
After the 10-day conference

68
00:06:08,561 --> 00:06:11,660
FDR reboarded the waiting seaplane

69
00:06:11,661 --> 00:06:13,900
and flew back to the United States

70
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:17,660
On their flight home

71
00:06:17,661 --> 00:06:21,380
the chief steward surprised the president with a frosted cake

72
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:24,160
to celebrate his 61st birthday

73
00:06:24,161 --> 00:06:29,880
The president's first airplane trip was a success

74
00:06:29,881 --> 00:06:33,620
The airplane had liberated the president

75
00:06:33,621 --> 00:06:36,860
and changed the presidency forever

76
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:49,060
The trip also gave birth to the idea that one airplane, a military plane, should be outfitted and set aside solely for use by the President.

77
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:58,360
The Army Air Force picked a C-87 Liberator Express, a transport version of the B-24 bomber.

78
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:03,900
It would be the first plane officially designated a presidential aircraft.

79
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:13,260
The C-87, like its cousin the B-24, had a very long range.

80
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:16,800
It also had another critical advantage.

81
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:24,160
When Franklin Roosevelt was in his 30s, he suffered an attack of polio,

82
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:26,920
and for the rest of his life, he needed a wheelchair.

83
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:35,020
The low door of the C-87 made it easier for the Secret Service agents to lift the president into the plane.

84
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:43,560
These rare color photos reveal the plane's one-of-a-kind interior.

85
00:07:43,561 --> 00:07:49,560
Four custom-built compartments could be converted into sleeping spaces.

86
00:07:49,561 --> 00:07:56,560
It also had a walk-in closet, two lavatories, and a kitchen galley with an electric stove.

87
00:07:56,561 --> 00:08:09,560
In June 1943, the President's C-87 joined the Air Transport Command's VIP Squadron, based at Washington's National Airport.

88
00:08:09,561 --> 00:08:19,560
ATC assigned Major Hank Myers as the pilot, and he selected his co-pilot, Lieutenant Elmer Smith.

89
00:08:19,561 --> 00:08:23,560
They nicknamed their new plane, the Guess Where To.

90
00:08:23,561 --> 00:08:30,449
2. But they never got a chance to fly FDR in his special transport.

91
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:32,300
The Secret Service

92
00:08:32,301 --> 00:08:38,127
learned that a C-87 had crashed because of mysterious structural vibrations.

93
00:08:38,128 --> 00:08:38,828
And they

94
00:08:38,829 --> 00:08:45,360
suggested the president avoid traveling in the Gasware 2. We were quite disappointed

95
00:08:45,361 --> 00:08:49,360
because we had trained for this flight, we had planned on it and everything, and then

96
00:08:49,361 --> 00:08:51,120
and all of a sudden we're set aside.

97
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:54,180
And it was a great disappointment, I'll be honest with you.

98
00:08:56,940 --> 00:09:00,120
Myers and Smith got a new assignment for the Guess Where To,

99
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:05,420
flying the First Lady on a goodwill tour of Allied bases in South America.

100
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:11,520
So we spent three weeks or a little more

101
00:09:11,521 --> 00:09:14,780
flying around the Caribbean and South America with Mrs. Roosevelt

102
00:09:14,781 --> 00:09:18,660
in the airplane that he wouldn't fly in, or wasn't permitted to fly in.

103
00:09:18,661 --> 00:09:21,880
So some people say, well, maybe there's a message there.

104
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:28,500
Myers and Smith flew nearly 13,000 miles with Mrs. Roosevelt.

105
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:32,640
They also flew senators and VIPs all over the world

106
00:09:32,641 --> 00:09:35,900
and never experienced any mysterious malfunctions.

107
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:38,720
But the Secret Service still wasn't convinced.

108
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:41,920
So in the summer of 1944,

109
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:45,260
the Air Force looked for a better plane to serve the president.

110
00:09:45,261 --> 00:09:51,980
and the guess where, too, made its final flight to an Arkansas scrapyard.

111
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:01,280
We now return to Air Force One.

112
00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:12,960
The Air Transport Command figured that a customized version of the reliable C-54 Skymaster

113
00:10:12,961 --> 00:10:16,380
would make a suitable replacement for the guest wearer, too,

114
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:19,100
and finally satisfy the Secret Service.

115
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:25,120
The Douglas Aircraft Company, based in Santa Monica, California,

116
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:30,160
outfitted one of their C-54s with all the trappings of executive privilege.

117
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:34,560
A big desk with an oversized leather chair,

118
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:39,800
a couch that converted into a bed with the push of a button,

119
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:42,380
and even a radio telephone.

120
00:10:42,960 --> 00:10:50,620
In the past, when FDR flew in a C-54,

121
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:55,020
extra-large boarding ramps had to be built to handle his wheelchair.

122
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:01,780
The sudden appearance of oversized ramps was a dead giveaway that FDR was about to arrive.

123
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:08,040
The Secret Service asked that the new C-54 carry a retractable elevator.

124
00:11:08,041 --> 00:11:16,020
this was battery operated it was wide enough to take his wheelchair roll him in and then raise him

125
00:11:16,021 --> 00:11:24,480
up to floor level in the cabin and he was in the airplane with no no fuss no bother at all

126
00:11:24,481 --> 00:11:32,660
in june 1944 after they returned from their south american trip with mrs roosevelt

127
00:11:32,661 --> 00:11:37,920
Major Myers and Lieutenant Smith were reassigned to the new aircraft.

128
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:44,680
For security reasons, the plane displayed no fancy names or presidential seals.

129
00:11:45,940 --> 00:11:49,820
It was known only by its tail number, 7451.

130
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:55,600
It was kept in a hangar at Washington's National Airport under constant guard.

131
00:11:56,660 --> 00:12:01,300
The extra security and special attention earned it the nickname Sacred Cow.

132
00:12:02,660 --> 00:12:13,460
In February 1945, Roosevelt planned to join Churchill and Stalin at a secret conference in the Russian city of Yalta.

133
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:19,660
FDR sailed to an airfield in the Mediterranean.

134
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:25,860
The sacred cow was waiting for him and flew him the rest of the way to Yalta.

135
00:12:25,861 --> 00:12:34,700
They brought him out in a wheelchair and put him in the elevator and hoisted him up into the airplane

136
00:12:34,701 --> 00:12:38,780
and then dropped him down again when we got into Russia.

137
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:45,560
As far as moving around in the airplane or anything like that, he did not move around because he was quite ill.

138
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:52,220
The short hops in and out of Yalta would be FDR's only trips aboard the Sacred Cow.

139
00:12:52,221 --> 00:12:56,540
Two months later, on April 12, 1945

140
00:12:56,541 --> 00:12:58,500
He passed away

141
00:12:58,501 --> 00:13:01,180
Although a reluctant flyer

142
00:13:01,181 --> 00:13:05,040
Franklin Roosevelt had opened the era of presidential flight

143
00:13:05,041 --> 00:13:12,120
Harry Truman inherited the sacred cow from his former boss

144
00:13:12,121 --> 00:13:16,240
Unlike FDR, Truman loved to fly

145
00:13:17,320 --> 00:13:21,780
He kept Hank Myers, Elmer Smith, and the sacred cow very busy

146
00:13:21,781 --> 00:13:27,920
He enjoyed coming up in the cockpit and either taking my seat or the other pilot's seat

147
00:13:27,921 --> 00:13:34,000
and sitting there for an hour or so and just looking out the window and chatting with us about any little thing

148
00:13:34,001 --> 00:13:37,520
and not having people bothering or anything to think about, just being friendly.

149
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:46,960
In 1947, the Douglas Aircraft Company rolled out their ultramodern DC-6.

150
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:50,180
It sported reversible pitch propellers.

151
00:13:51,780 --> 00:13:55,260
A pressurized cabin, which meant greater comfort for the passengers.

152
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:59,520
And the latest in aircraft instrumentation, radar.

153
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:05,940
The Air Force bought the state-of-the-art airliner to replace the aging sacred cow.

154
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:12,300
Rather than allowing the press to name the airplane,

155
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:15,400
the Office of the Presidential Pilot felt,

156
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:19,520
we'd better have a jump start and put our own name on the airplane.

157
00:14:19,521 --> 00:14:22,320
They selected the name Independence.

158
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:29,200
This was not only the hometown of Truman, but it also had a national flavor to it.

159
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:36,460
The stylized Eagle paint scheme, considered too extravagant for the government's lean budget,

160
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:39,160
was a gift of the Douglas Aircraft Company.

161
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:48,600
The Independence, officially a C-118, made presidential flight more convenient.

162
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:55,520
It offered a larger stateroom for the president that converted into a more comfortable bedroom.

163
00:14:55,521 --> 00:14:59,520
The cabin had room for an additional 25 passengers.

164
00:14:59,521 --> 00:15:04,520
And a fully stocked kitchen galley could prepare meals for all on board.

165
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:12,520
Even with the most modern airliner and years of experience,

166
00:15:12,521 --> 00:15:17,520
the crew still faced the pressure of flying the world's most influential leader.

167
00:15:17,521 --> 00:15:23,160
Senators and secretaries would come down and get the crew aside now.

168
00:15:23,260 --> 00:15:26,860
You boys be careful because you've got the president on board and he's the only one we have.

169
00:15:27,340 --> 00:15:29,340
Well, we look at it differently.

170
00:15:29,740 --> 00:15:33,760
He's lucky to be flying with us because we like life and we're going to come home, boy.

171
00:15:33,820 --> 00:15:34,520
I believe it.

172
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:36,760
I'm not going to do anything stupid up there.

173
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:45,660
Myers and Smith flew Truman so often that their special tricks of the trade became standard procedure.

174
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:49,800
In effect, they wrote the book on VIP travel.

175
00:15:51,860 --> 00:15:55,660
We always padded our flight plan with 10 or 15 minutes extra,

176
00:15:56,100 --> 00:15:59,540
just in case we ran into winds or some unforecast thing,

177
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:03,920
so we could either throttle back and kill the time off,

178
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:05,660
so we'd always land right on the dot.

179
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:12,820
In October 1950, Truman flew to the tiny South Pacific island of Wake.

180
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:18,300
He went there to discuss the progress of the Korean War with General Douglas MacArthur.

181
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:22,140
It was a brief, though remarkable, visit.

182
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:26,260
It set the stage for the eventual clash between Truman and the General.

183
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:31,160
And at 7,000 miles, it was the longest air journey of the Truman presidency.

184
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:37,040
During Harry Truman's busy seven years in office,

185
00:16:37,940 --> 00:16:42,160
the chief executive's aircraft became a working extension of the Oval Office.

186
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:54,020
On January 20th, 1953, Dwight D. Eisenhower was sworn in as the 34th U.S. President.

187
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:02,020
Eisenhower exercised his presidential prerogative and selected a new plane and a new crew.

188
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:07,040
Hank Myers had already left the Air Force and returned to American Airlines.

189
00:17:07,041 --> 00:17:11,980
and Elmer Smith, who served as the president's co-pilot for 10 years,

190
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:16,000
took a desk job in the Air Force and turned in his presidential wings.

191
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:23,460
The new president favored the sleek Lockheed Constellation,

192
00:17:23,461 --> 00:17:26,360
in military jargon, a C-121.

193
00:17:29,020 --> 00:17:32,760
Ike selected a Constellation already in the VIP fleet in Washington.

194
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:35,360
Tail number 8610.

195
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:42,180
He named it the Columbine, after the state flower of his wife's native Colorado.

196
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:49,000
Standard procedure in the Air Force was to designate a flight by its tail number.

197
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:56,520
In 1953, this led to a frightening case of mistaken identity during radio communication

198
00:17:56,521 --> 00:18:00,500
between the President's plane and Eastern Airlines flight

199
00:18:00,501 --> 00:18:04,000
and the Washington Air Traffic Control Center in Richmond.

200
00:18:04,001 --> 00:18:09,840
Eisenhower's co-pilot, Colonel Bill Thomas, was on the radio for that fateful call.

201
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:18,960
I called in saying, Air Force 8610, Richmond 12,000 feet.

202
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:26,980
And the center came back and said, Roger 610, report at 15.

203
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:33,640
And about two or three seconds later, another voice came on and said,

204
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,740
Senator, did you call Eastern 610?

205
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:42,700
And Senator came back and said, no, I was working an Air Force airplane.

206
00:18:45,620 --> 00:18:51,420
The mix-up between Eastern Airlines 610 and Air Force 610 was too close for comfort.

207
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:59,580
So they came up with the idea that any airplane that the president was in

208
00:18:59,581 --> 00:19:03,180
would henceforth be designated as Air Force One.

209
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,400
regardless of its tail number or nickname,

210
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:12,360
from the moment the president came aboard to the minute he departed,

211
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:17,340
that plane would carry the radio call sign, Air Force One.

212
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:28,980
On November 24th, 1954,

213
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:33,140
the Air Force proudly welcomed the newest member of the presidential fleet,

214
00:19:33,141 --> 00:19:37,480
a Lockheed Super Constellation, tale number 7885.

215
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:44,580
First Lady Mamie Eisenhower christened the new aircraft Columbine 3,

216
00:19:45,140 --> 00:19:49,240
not with champagne, but with a bottle of Colorado Rocky Mountain Spring water.

217
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:57,380
The sleek, graceful Super Constellation designated VC-121

218
00:19:57,381 --> 00:20:01,080
was bigger, faster, and offered greater range than its predecessor.

219
00:20:02,040 --> 00:20:05,780
Columbine 3 represented the state-of-the-art in VIP travel.

220
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:09,900
Full galley facilities, sophisticated instrument landing systems,

221
00:20:10,320 --> 00:20:14,020
radar altimeter, air-to-ground telephone, and secure teletype.

222
00:20:18,840 --> 00:20:23,320
President Eisenhower was himself a private pilot, and he loved to fly.

223
00:20:23,900 --> 00:20:28,680
While in office, Ike logged more than 30,000 miles a year as a passenger.

224
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:38,920
One of the reasons that Eisenhower liked it was that it presented him with an absolutely uninterruptible meeting place.

225
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:49,360
If he had certain things that he had to do, he'd get the people necessary that would work on that project and get them on the airplane.

226
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:52,680
And he knew that there wasn't going to be any interruption.

227
00:20:52,681 --> 00:21:00,260
Eisenhower appreciated the value of Air Force One as a powerful tool of Cold War politics.

228
00:21:01,480 --> 00:21:07,060
He came into the office having been a world traveler.

229
00:21:07,300 --> 00:21:13,560
He knew the value of face-to-face confrontation when problems came up.

230
00:21:13,561 --> 00:21:20,460
I think that he changed the complexion of world politics because of that.

231
00:21:20,461 --> 00:21:27,040
Columbine 3 was a beautiful aircraft, but it was still propeller-driven.

232
00:21:27,540 --> 00:21:31,440
By the late 50s, the Soviets were arriving at conferences in jets.

233
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:35,060
America was unwilling to accept this loss of prestige.

234
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:41,080
In 1958, the Boeing 707 was chosen as the next presidential plane.

235
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:47,000
Three 707s were modified for the Special Air Missions, or SAM Squadron.

236
00:21:47,001 --> 00:21:51,340
Tail numbers 970, 971, and 972.

237
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:58,600
On May 12, 1959, the Air Force took delivery of the first new jet at Boeing's Seattle plant.

238
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:05,220
On its flight back to Washington, it set a coast-to-coast speed record, just 3 hours and 44 minutes.

239
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:16,060
In December, President Eisenhower embarked on an unprecedented international tour, 11 countries in just 18 days.

240
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:26,220
During Ike's final year in office, the dazzling speed of the jets inspired him to increase his air mileage from 30,000 to nearly 80,000 miles.

241
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:33,640
In the spring of 1960, Ike scheduled a trip to Russia.

242
00:22:34,580 --> 00:22:38,200
Secretly, aircraft 970 was specially modified for the mission.

243
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:46,720
John Foster Dulles, who was Secretary of State, and his brother Alan Dulles, who was a director of the CIA,

244
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:54,180
for Eisenhower had this great idea they would equip Air Force One with the same

245
00:22:54,181 --> 00:22:58,860
cameras that were being used on the U-2 spy planes which were tremendous

246
00:22:58,861 --> 00:23:04,780
cameras they could at 50,000 feet they could read a you know a license plate on

247
00:23:04,781 --> 00:23:08,860
an automobile and they wanted to do it particularly because they wanted to be

248
00:23:08,861 --> 00:23:15,600
able to take pictures of all the Soviet military installations on the ground

249
00:23:15,601 --> 00:23:18,440
However, the mission was never carried out.

250
00:23:21,180 --> 00:23:27,120
On May 1st, 1960, the Russians shot down an American U-2 spy plane.

251
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:32,980
The embarrassing incident canceled Ike's trip, and the spy cameras were removed.

252
00:23:35,780 --> 00:23:41,580
By the time John Kennedy took office, most presidential trips were aboard the three jets.

253
00:23:41,581 --> 00:23:46,160
with aircraft 970 designated as the primary plane.

254
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:51,260
But the Air Force had ordered a new long-range 707.

255
00:23:52,660 --> 00:23:55,580
The Kennedys wanted a fresh look for the new aircraft.

256
00:23:56,120 --> 00:23:59,240
Instead of the military's high-visibility day-glow orange,

257
00:23:59,241 --> 00:24:03,160
they favored something more stylish and more national in character.

258
00:24:05,640 --> 00:24:10,300
Noted industrial designer Raymond Lowy was commissioned to redesign the exterior.

259
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:14,580
The results were the elegant paint scheme that remains to this day.

260
00:24:16,900 --> 00:24:19,600
The new 707 was magnificent.

261
00:24:20,300 --> 00:24:24,100
The first jet designed and built specifically for presidential travel.

262
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:32,000
She could cruise at over 620 miles per hour and fly non-stop for more than 7,000 miles.

263
00:24:32,001 --> 00:24:35,820
Some 2,500 miles more than the old 707s.

264
00:24:36,940 --> 00:24:40,020
She had an impressive array of luxury amenities.

265
00:24:40,300 --> 00:24:44,920
A spacious stateroom with sleeping accommodations for the president and first lady.

266
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:48,120
First class seating for up to 50 additional passengers.

267
00:24:49,140 --> 00:24:54,440
Sophisticated communications that allowed the president secure telephone calls anywhere in the world.

268
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:59,960
And the plane even offered the ability to cook fresh meals from scratch right on board.

269
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:08,640
But when tail number 26000 was first delivered, it was lacking one notable feature.

270
00:25:08,641 --> 00:25:10,280
the presidential seal.

271
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:15,800
Colonel James Swindoll, Kennedy's Air Force One pilot,

272
00:25:16,160 --> 00:25:17,380
recommended a modification.

273
00:25:18,640 --> 00:25:22,320
We went back through the Air Force and the White House

274
00:25:22,321 --> 00:25:24,500
to suggest a seal be put on it

275
00:25:24,501 --> 00:25:28,160
because we wanted to reserve that airplane for the president

276
00:25:28,161 --> 00:25:29,660
as much as possible.

277
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:31,540
And with that seal on it,

278
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:35,160
either the president had to approve who used it

279
00:25:35,161 --> 00:25:36,540
or he had to use it.

280
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:42,140
The new plane emerged as the gleaming symbol of America,

281
00:25:42,740 --> 00:25:45,440
matching the dynamic image of the nation's young president.

282
00:25:46,980 --> 00:25:48,720
JFK loved Air Force One.

283
00:25:49,400 --> 00:25:50,540
His travels were memorable,

284
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,340
but he was only able to enjoy his flying White House for a brief time.

285
00:25:55,560 --> 00:26:00,020
The plane was just over a year old when it carried him to Dallas, Texas.

286
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:05,400
On the morning of November 22, 1963,

287
00:26:06,060 --> 00:26:08,460
President Kennedy arrived at Love Field.

288
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:21,020
The horrible events of the next few hours would focus the eyes of the world on Dallas and Air Force One.

289
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:29,440
At one o'clock in the afternoon, America's 35th president was pronounced dead.

290
00:26:30,560 --> 00:26:37,520
Fearing a wider conspiracy, the Secret Service rushed Vice President Lyndon Johnson to the security of Air Force One.

291
00:26:38,480 --> 00:26:41,380
I met him at the foot of the stairs, and I addressed him as Mr. President,

292
00:26:41,500 --> 00:26:45,320
because I knew at that time that President Kennedy had already passed away.

293
00:26:45,560 --> 00:26:50,400
And when he came on board, he asked us to close the window shades,

294
00:26:50,420 --> 00:26:52,840
just in case somebody wanted to take a shot at him.

295
00:26:55,140 --> 00:26:59,640
Immediately, preparations were made to fly the slain President back to Washington.

296
00:27:00,780 --> 00:27:04,600
It didn't seem proper for the casket to ride in the baggage area,

297
00:27:04,601 --> 00:27:09,320
so seats and partitions were removed to make room in the passenger compartment.

298
00:27:11,260 --> 00:27:15,560
At 2.37 p.m., Lyndon Johnson was sworn in.

299
00:27:16,340 --> 00:27:18,660
Jackie Kennedy stood bravely at his side,

300
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:21,700
her clothes still splattered with her husband's blood.

301
00:27:24,040 --> 00:27:29,800
Just ten minutes later, Air Force One was airborne, heading home to Washington.

302
00:27:29,801 --> 00:27:36,180
An aircraft filled with stunned silence and paralyzing grief.

303
00:27:59,800 --> 00:28:04,640
In addition to Air Force One's own security,

304
00:28:05,040 --> 00:28:10,380
each state's Air National Guard is assigned as extra security for Air Force One.

305
00:28:15,340 --> 00:28:18,800
Aircraft 26000 was never given any nickname.

306
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:21,940
The plane itself became known as Air Force One.

307
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:28,340
But still, any Air Force plane the President boards takes on the Air Force One call sign.

308
00:28:28,341 --> 00:28:33,340
If the president flies in a Marine aircraft, it becomes Marine One.

309
00:28:33,940 --> 00:28:35,560
Army, Army One.

310
00:28:36,360 --> 00:28:40,560
If the vice president is flying, the call sign becomes Air Force Two.

311
00:28:41,220 --> 00:28:47,440
But if the passenger is the first lady or any other VIP, the flight is designated just by the tail number.

312
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:53,400
In 1972, another Boeing 707 was added to the presidential fleet.

313
00:28:53,401 --> 00:28:58,180
A virtual twin of 26,000, tail number 27,000.

314
00:28:58,340 --> 00:29:05,660
These two aircraft were continually upgraded and together served as Air Force One until 1990.

315
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:14,100
With each change in administration, the plane took on a new personality, symbolic of the policies and the style of each president.

316
00:29:16,200 --> 00:29:21,220
The most spectacular there was Lyndon Johnson's throne room, as we called it.

317
00:29:21,221 --> 00:29:27,140
He had a special chair built that would raise up and down at a push of a button

318
00:29:27,141 --> 00:29:34,820
so that he could be always at least at eye level or higher than anybody else seated in the compartment with him.

319
00:29:36,840 --> 00:29:40,860
He also installed a table that could electronically raise and lower

320
00:29:40,861 --> 00:29:46,960
to be used as a conference table, a coffee table, or just to make visitors feel small.

321
00:29:46,961 --> 00:29:51,840
President Johnson was a very gregarious human being

322
00:29:51,841 --> 00:29:54,980
and when he talked he wanted everybody to pay attention to him

323
00:29:54,981 --> 00:29:59,420
so they then put in plexiglass bulkheads in the airplane

324
00:29:59,421 --> 00:30:03,500
now he could stand in the front of the airplane and look down through the whole tube

325
00:30:03,501 --> 00:30:06,480
and talk to everybody at the same time which he liked to do

326
00:30:06,481 --> 00:30:11,220
and as a matter of fact he liked it so much that they used to have little microphones

327
00:30:11,820 --> 00:30:15,420
stashed away at places in the airplane so that if he says

328
00:30:15,421 --> 00:30:16,380
hey, get me a microphone.

329
00:30:16,820 --> 00:30:18,820
The steward, all he had to do was reach up here in this compartment,

330
00:30:18,960 --> 00:30:19,960
pull it out and hand it to him.

331
00:30:19,980 --> 00:30:21,700
And he was on the public address system.

332
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:23,820
He just wanted people to pay attention to him.

333
00:30:26,420 --> 00:30:29,940
Richard Nixon restored the aircraft to a more traditional layout

334
00:30:29,941 --> 00:30:31,660
and a more reserved demeanor.

335
00:30:32,720 --> 00:30:36,780
He usually stayed in his stateroom alone or with a few advisors.

336
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:39,700
But his pilot, Colonel Ralph Albertazzi,

337
00:30:39,701 --> 00:30:43,800
remembers the flight crew often had to tend to Nixon's mechanical needs.

338
00:30:43,801 --> 00:30:48,920
there's a kind of a joke among the stewards how quickly were they going to get called

339
00:30:48,921 --> 00:30:52,140
straighten up something that he had screwed up you know he couldn't get the seat right or he

340
00:30:52,141 --> 00:30:55,800
couldn't turn turn on the radio or he couldn't watch the television or he didn't know how to

341
00:30:55,801 --> 00:31:00,280
work the telephone or those kind of things he was we called him mechanically disinclined

342
00:31:00,281 --> 00:31:09,140
nixon's tenure aboard air force one will be best remembered for his historic trips to the soviet

343
00:31:09,141 --> 00:31:10,560
Union and China.

344
00:31:10,561 --> 00:31:17,480
And of course, the dramatic transition of power after his resignation in 1974.

345
00:31:19,180 --> 00:31:25,200
During Nixon's final trip aboard the plane, Vice President Ford would be sworn in in Washington.

346
00:31:25,960 --> 00:31:31,400
We now have a president who's going to leave Washington, D.C., and en route to California,

347
00:31:31,700 --> 00:31:33,055
become the ex-president.

348
00:31:33,056 --> 00:31:36,780
So we had coordinated with the Federal Aviation Administration and

349
00:31:36,781 --> 00:31:38,280
the other ground support agencies.

350
00:31:38,660 --> 00:31:44,460
At the moment that the Vice President, Jerry Ford, would say, I do, to the oath of office,

351
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:49,300
we would change our call sign from Air Force One to Sam 27,000.

352
00:31:49,880 --> 00:31:51,280
And that's the way it happened.

353
00:31:51,400 --> 00:31:54,400
And it happened over Missouri at 39,000 feet.

354
00:31:56,060 --> 00:32:02,060
Lee Simmons was an Air Force One steward, and he recalls visiting Nixon in his stateroom

355
00:32:02,061 --> 00:32:03,640
right after the change in power.

356
00:32:03,641 --> 00:32:13,880
I went into his compartment to say I'm very sorry that you're going to be leaving the presidency

357
00:32:13,881 --> 00:32:20,320
and let him know how much I appreciate how much he has done for me but before I could really

358
00:32:20,321 --> 00:32:27,060
console him he was consoling me by telling me you know everything's going to be okay

359
00:32:27,061 --> 00:32:33,100
you take care of your family and you hang in there and let life continue as it is and don't

360
00:32:33,101 --> 00:32:34,280
let it get you down.

361
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:39,340
Well, I walked out of there with my head down and with tears in my eyes

362
00:32:39,341 --> 00:32:42,320
because I was not prepared for that.

363
00:32:42,620 --> 00:32:45,400
I wanted to go in and say something comforting to him.

364
00:32:45,600 --> 00:32:47,440
It turned out that he was trying to comfort me.

365
00:32:49,440 --> 00:32:51,620
At the end of Nixon's last flight,

366
00:32:51,621 --> 00:32:55,880
he requested a group photo with his dedicated Air Force One crew.

367
00:32:55,881 --> 00:33:04,060
Gerald Ford's sudden ascendance to the presidency

368
00:33:04,061 --> 00:33:06,980
left him little time to modify Air Force One

369
00:33:06,981 --> 00:33:11,240
My wife Betty and I made no changes

370
00:33:11,241 --> 00:33:15,580
Of course, we came in rather unexpectedly

371
00:33:15,581 --> 00:33:19,700
and with not any real forewarning

372
00:33:19,701 --> 00:33:22,460
and when we took over

373
00:33:22,461 --> 00:33:25,120
we had a lot more important things to do

374
00:33:25,121 --> 00:33:29,400
than to fool around with any changes in Air Force One.

375
00:33:33,240 --> 00:33:37,620
Whenever I flew on Air Force One, I had my staff there.

376
00:33:37,780 --> 00:33:40,360
I had the communications capability.

377
00:33:41,360 --> 00:33:44,700
Secretary of State Kissinger would be along,

378
00:33:44,701 --> 00:33:49,920
and we would discuss what was on the agenda in this country

379
00:33:49,921 --> 00:33:52,040
where we were going for meetings.

380
00:33:52,041 --> 00:33:55,500
so Air Force One was a working office.

381
00:33:56,680 --> 00:34:00,380
For President Ford, Air Force One offered sanctuary

382
00:34:00,381 --> 00:34:05,260
after the assassination attempt by Sarah Jane Moore in San Francisco in 1975.

383
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:10,200
Well, after Sarah Jane Moore took the shot,

384
00:34:10,480 --> 00:34:14,320
the Secret Service jammed me into the limousine

385
00:34:14,321 --> 00:34:18,760
and we drove literally, I'm told, 90 miles an hour

386
00:34:18,761 --> 00:34:22,100
from the center of San Francisco out to the airport.

387
00:34:22,840 --> 00:34:26,080
So we got to the airport ahead of Mrs. Ford.

388
00:34:27,100 --> 00:34:32,100
Normally she would be on board and I would get on and we would take off.

389
00:34:32,500 --> 00:34:33,960
But we got there early.

390
00:34:34,900 --> 00:34:41,360
So she lands and she notices all the excitement around Air Force One

391
00:34:41,361 --> 00:34:43,080
but doesn't know what happened.

392
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:54,580
She walks into the lounge in the aircraft, Air Force One, and blithely says, well, how did they treat you in San Francisco?

393
00:35:02,260 --> 00:35:07,400
President Jimmy Carter wanted the airplane to reflect the character of his entire administration.

394
00:35:07,401 --> 00:35:14,640
leaner budgets, austerity programs, and no free souvenirs on Air Force One.

395
00:35:16,260 --> 00:35:23,660
They were trying to get the message through that they weren't going to be an extravagant, overspending White House.

396
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:29,260
A lot of things were cut throughout the administration, but as far as the airplane went,

397
00:35:29,980 --> 00:35:37,340
we did away with all the freebies, would not accept anything from the soft drink companies, cigarette companies.

398
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:38,400
etc.

399
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:40,160
And it's kind of

400
00:35:40,161 --> 00:35:42,000
bare bones.

401
00:35:46,760 --> 00:35:48,280
Ronald and Nancy Reagan

402
00:35:48,281 --> 00:35:49,980
brought back some of the amenities

403
00:35:49,981 --> 00:35:50,840
to the airplane.

404
00:35:51,380 --> 00:35:53,460
But still, Air Force One remained

405
00:35:53,461 --> 00:35:54,460
a working office.

406
00:35:56,400 --> 00:35:57,700
You got on the plane

407
00:35:57,701 --> 00:35:59,580
and usually immediately went to work.

408
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:01,180
There were always speeches to write,

409
00:36:02,020 --> 00:36:03,360
things to research,

410
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:05,660
phone calls to be made back to Washington.

411
00:36:05,661 --> 00:36:10,300
There was none of this trade tables and your upright position stuff.

412
00:36:10,460 --> 00:36:17,780
I mean, sometimes we'd be landing and you'd be standing at a Xerox machine peeling off the last pages of the president's speech.

413
00:36:19,920 --> 00:36:27,340
Marlon Fitzwater flew extensively with both Presidents Reagan and Bush and was able to gain a special insight into the two men.

414
00:36:29,560 --> 00:36:33,240
There was an informality about long flights on the plane.

415
00:36:33,241 --> 00:36:37,700
President Bush and President Reagan both would usually change into comfortable clothes,

416
00:36:37,800 --> 00:36:41,760
maybe jogging suits or casual clothes of some kind.

417
00:36:42,120 --> 00:36:47,140
And it was kind of a rare opportunity to see the president at ease, if you will,

418
00:36:47,180 --> 00:36:50,720
and to hear what he thought and to hear how he thought and how he reasoned.

419
00:36:50,740 --> 00:36:53,980
And a lot of staff people never get that opportunity when you're in the White House.

420
00:37:03,240 --> 00:37:13,420
During the Cold War, we had several summit meetings in the Soviet Union or visits to China and other places where we knew that our rooms were bugged.

421
00:37:13,421 --> 00:37:16,060
We knew people were listening to us all the time.

422
00:37:16,720 --> 00:37:19,000
And so getting back on the plane was such a safe haven.

423
00:37:19,680 --> 00:37:24,120
And you felt like, I'm back in the loving arms of America again. Just get me home.

424
00:37:33,240 --> 00:37:41,300
In the late 1980s, the most impressive aircraft to bear the call sign Air Force One

425
00:37:41,301 --> 00:37:45,840
came together inside this cavernous Boeing factory in Everett, Washington.

426
00:37:47,880 --> 00:37:56,040
Piece by piece, this 747-200B grew into a flying machine of unparalleled capability.

427
00:37:56,041 --> 00:38:04,520
At first glance, it's a typical jumbo jet, six stories tall and 231 feet long.

428
00:38:05,500 --> 00:38:07,480
But that's where the similarities end.

429
00:38:09,540 --> 00:38:17,220
The 747, tail number 28000, came off the assembly line protected by an anti-corrosive coating

430
00:38:17,221 --> 00:38:22,420
and flew to the Boeing Modification Center in Wichita, Kansas.

431
00:38:22,421 --> 00:38:30,420
Technicians in Wichita fitted the plane with equipment you won't find on standard 747s,

432
00:38:30,421 --> 00:38:38,420
like two retractable stairways and a self-contained baggage loading ramp.

433
00:38:38,421 --> 00:38:45,420
Cabinet makers hand-crafted each piece of interior furniture.

434
00:38:56,563 --> 00:39:04,777
Boeing also added some equipment normally reserved for combat planes, like ECM, or electronic countermeasures, that jam enemy radar and alert the crew to

435
00:39:04,778 --> 00:39:10,200
surface-to-air missiles, and dispensers that eject flares to confuse and divert heat-seeking weapons.

436
00:39:15,420 --> 00:39:23,680
On the flight back to the Boeing factory, the crew practiced one of the plane's unique capabilities, in-flight refueling.

437
00:39:24,540 --> 00:39:32,740
The ability to take fuel from an Air Force tanker jet means that this plane, at least theoretically, can remain airborne indefinitely.

438
00:39:32,741 --> 00:39:46,520
After getting its famous blue and white paint job, the plane flew from Boeing to its new home at Andrews Air Force Base in Washington, D.C.

439
00:39:49,320 --> 00:39:53,240
Hey, Danny. How are you? Well, here we are.

440
00:39:53,241 --> 00:40:01,180
On September 6, 1990, President George Bush came aboard for his first trip in the new plane,

441
00:40:01,880 --> 00:40:05,780
and tail number 28000 officially became Air Force One.

442
00:40:07,880 --> 00:40:08,840
Isn't this beautiful?

443
00:40:09,460 --> 00:40:10,200
See the hot tub?

444
00:40:12,680 --> 00:40:22,740
It was really magical, because no one had ever seen a plane that big or that luxurious or outfitted the way Air Force One was.

445
00:40:22,741 --> 00:40:32,121
You couldn't be prepared for this conference room that was as big as the conference room in the White House for the luxury of the seating and for an aisle that went

446
00:40:32,122 --> 00:40:33,780
the full length of the plane.

447
00:40:33,920 --> 00:40:38,800
And you felt like you were flying in the Waldorf Astoria when you were when you were flying on the plane.

448
00:40:39,400 --> 00:40:44,140
And that first flight, I remember just sitting here thinking, this can't this can't be real.

449
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:48,700
The plane is so big and constructed in such a way that you can walk around.

450
00:40:49,160 --> 00:40:51,260
There's a lot of interaction between people.

451
00:40:51,261 --> 00:40:56,120
And so you just lose that whole kind of sense of being cooped up someplace.

452
00:40:56,300 --> 00:40:58,720
It virtually eliminates jet lag.

453
00:41:00,540 --> 00:41:03,220
4,000 square feet of floor space.

454
00:41:03,820 --> 00:41:07,300
Make this the largest airborne White House to serve a president.

455
00:41:08,900 --> 00:41:13,320
It can accommodate 70 passengers and 26 crew members.

456
00:41:15,020 --> 00:41:18,820
Two kitchens can dish out up to 100 meals at a time.

457
00:41:18,821 --> 00:41:23,240
Freezers can store enough food to feed everybody for a week.

458
00:41:25,680 --> 00:41:35,180
It has 85 telephones, 19 TV monitors, 11 VCRs, and top-secret satellite communications equipment.

459
00:41:36,680 --> 00:41:39,980
The first time you go on Air Force One, you want to call your mom.

460
00:41:39,981 --> 00:41:44,740
And the grandness of it is that the Air Force One operator comes on the phone and says,

461
00:41:44,741 --> 00:41:45,619
You know, Mrs.

462
00:41:45,620 --> 00:41:50,140
Fitzwater, I have your son calling from 33,000 feet over western Kansas.

463
00:41:50,280 --> 00:41:51,180
Will you take the call?

464
00:41:51,580 --> 00:41:54,280
She says, of course not, you fool, and hangs up.

465
00:41:54,700 --> 00:41:55,920
And then you call back again.

466
00:41:57,580 --> 00:42:01,060
There are actually two presidential 747s.

467
00:42:01,560 --> 00:42:05,060
With the exception of the tail number, they're identical twins.

468
00:42:05,800 --> 00:42:09,760
Often, the second aircraft will follow the first as an emergency backup.

469
00:42:10,360 --> 00:42:13,080
They're known officially as VC-25s.

470
00:42:13,081 --> 00:42:16,680
And the price tag for both planes, $300 million.

471
00:42:19,300 --> 00:42:24,900
They're looked after by 140 hand-picked specialists of the Presidential Pilot's Office,

472
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:27,200
part of the 89th Airlift Wing.

473
00:42:30,220 --> 00:42:32,540
Security receives the highest priority.

474
00:42:33,400 --> 00:42:39,900
Even the maintenance people work in pairs to make sure no person operating alone has access to the plane.

475
00:42:40,820 --> 00:42:44,020
Because of the unpredictability of presidential schedules,

476
00:42:44,540 --> 00:42:47,800
the crews keep the planes ready to fly within a moment's notice.

477
00:42:49,620 --> 00:42:52,600
Being part of the elite presidential support team

478
00:42:52,601 --> 00:42:55,680
means these people are the best at what they do.

479
00:42:56,720 --> 00:42:58,860
The people that you see maintaining the aircraft,

480
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:01,000
the people that you see flying our aircraft,

481
00:43:01,100 --> 00:43:03,060
are probably the best troops that we have in the Air Force.

482
00:43:03,520 --> 00:43:07,460
We go through a very highly selective process to hire them into the 89th,

483
00:43:07,461 --> 00:43:11,280
and then we give them the extra training that brings them up to the standards that we need day to day,

484
00:43:11,760 --> 00:43:14,000
and they are ready to go whenever the President needs them.

485
00:43:15,500 --> 00:43:22,280
Air Force One doesn't carry an emergency presidential parachute or hidden escape pod for the chief executive.

486
00:43:23,240 --> 00:43:29,500
Special security forces guard the plane constantly and even travel with the President aboard his aircraft.

487
00:43:37,460 --> 00:43:42,520
When the president travels overseas,

488
00:43:43,160 --> 00:43:46,520
many countries offer Air Force One the honor of a fighter escort.

489
00:43:47,160 --> 00:43:51,240
But an escort raises the risk of a mid-air collision with a foreign fighter plane,

490
00:43:51,241 --> 00:43:53,820
and the offers are respectfully turned down.

491
00:43:55,760 --> 00:43:59,880
What was once a rare and very risky part of the president's job

492
00:43:59,881 --> 00:44:06,020
has become a necessary, safe, and very much envied aspect of the presidency.

493
00:44:06,021 --> 00:44:16,400
For nearly 50 years, Air Force One has remained the clearest demonstration that the most powerful man in the world has arrived.

494
